@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
allowmixing
hide circle
hide empty methods
title G3 · Access alternatives: two ways to call the synthetic read_count getter
top to bottom direction
class "read_count\nThe getter the caller wants to use" as getter {
  example::objects, version 1
  Either path implements its whole contract
}
class "objc_like\nSend a message to the counter" as message {
  Send currentCount
  Use the object-message convention
  {field} identity mapping: receiver becomes message target
  stable: use the fixture's compatibility guarantee
}
class "swift_like\nCompile a call from source" as source {
  Call Outer.Counter.currentCount.get
  Use the source convention
  identity mapping: preserve the declared operation
  source: build with the fixture-build recipe
}
$catalogue(abi, "native · Synthetic ABI entry\nexample.fixture-abi, version 1\nSynthetic digest: sha256: + 64 b digits")
$catalogue(profile, "fixture · Synthetic profile entry\nexample.fixture, version 1\nSynthetic digest: sha256: + 64 a digits")
getter --> message : offers this path in test
getter --> source : offers this path in test
message --> abi : reads the object-message\ncalling rules here
source --> abi : reads the source\ncalling rules here
message --> profile : reads how to send\nthe message here
source --> profile : reads how to compile\nthe source call here
note top of getter
  A tool chooses a path explicitly or through its configuration policy.
  Listing a path first gives it no priority.
end note
note bottom of profile
  test names target fixture64, platform fixture 1
  and toolchain fixture-compiler 1.
  fixture-build specifies how to build the source call.
  Separately built binaries need their own compatibility guarantee.
end note
legend bottom
  Invented API and synthetic definitions. Non-normative: A2, A4, F1.
  Compartmented box = contract item; cylinder = catalogue definition.
  Arrows connect each choice to the definitions a tool reads; they do not order calls.
  Both paths select one ABI entry, choosing different conventions within it.
  Native calling algorithms and real Objective-C or Swift support are outside this view.
endlegend
@enduml
